<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louvre Abu Dhabi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2017">2017</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> Abu Dhabi is an art and civilisation museum on Saadiyat Island in Abu Dhabi, opened in November 2017 under a 2007 agreement between the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> that lent it the Louvre's name. Designed by Jean Nouvel beneath a vast perforated dome, it displays works from across world history and is a flagship of the emirate's cultural ambitions.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 2005 the United Arab Emirates proposed to the French government a museum in the Emirates bearing the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. Talks began in June 2005, when a delegation from Abu Dhabi visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and formal negotiations started in the summer of 2006. In 2006 the Abu Dhabi authorities chose Jean Nouvel as architect, a choice first suggested by Thomas Krens of the Solomon R.</p><p>Guggenheim Foundation. The project was revealed by Le Monde in September 2006 and met controversy, including a petition by the curators Françoise Cachin, Jean Clair and Roland Recht. The agreement was signed on 6 March 2007 and ratified by the French Parliament on 9 October 2007.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Construction began on Saadiyat Island on 26 May 2009. The opening, first expected in 2012, was postponed, and the museum was inaugurated on 8 November 2017 by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-macron">Emmanuel Macron</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Mohammed bin Zayed Al Nahyan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-bin-rashid-al-maktoum">Mohammed bin Rashid Al Maktoum</a>. It opened to the public three days later. Before then the collection was shown in preview exhibitions: an exhibition at the Emirates Palace in 2009 presented the first 19 acquisitions, among them a Mamluk Quran, a Virgin and Child by Bellini and a Mondrian, and Birth of a Museum in 2013 showed 130 works.</p><p>Nouvel designed a dome of 7,850 aluminium stars in eight layers, meant to recall sunlight through date palm fronds, over a museum of 55 buildings, 23 of them galleries, with tidal pools in the galleries to suggest a museum in the sea. Two public works were commissioned for the opening: a limestone sculpture by Jenny Holzer engraved with texts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, Arabic and French, and an installation by Giuseppe Penone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The museum is overseen by the Abu Dhabi Department of Culture &amp; Tourism and linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by the 2007 agreement, which was extended in 2023 for an additional payment of €165 million. A French company, Agence France-Muséums, was incorporated on 23 August 2007, with shareholders that include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> and other French cultural institutions. Under the agreement Abu Dhabi pays €400 million for the use of the Louvre name, as well as sums for art loans, special exhibitions and management advice.</p><p>Critics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, led by the art historian Didier Rykner, opposed the deal, and a petition against it was signed by 4,650 curators, archaeologists and art historians. Human Rights Watch reported the confiscation of workers' passports during construction, and Nouvel defended the workers' conditions as better than in some European countries. Others have criticised a European focus in the modern and contemporary collections.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louvre Abu Dhabi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cultural and scientific projects such as the <b>Louvre Abu Dhabi</b>, opened in 2017, the Hope probe, which reached Mars orbit in 2021, and the hosting of Expo 2020 and of the COP28 climate conference in 2023 projected the image of a modern, outward-looking state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
